#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171823 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "019e5a17-1aab-565b-8881-447c86a9a7ec" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Fields on Two-Dimensions"}] :question/id #uuid "bccee2b9-f937-5df5-b3cf-4e45096c9ac3" :question/math-academy-id "e-9465" :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on $\\mathbb R^2$?\n\n1. $\\mathbf A(x,y) = \\sqrt{x}\\,\\mathbf i + y^3\\,\\mathbf j$\n2. $\\mathbf B(x,y,z) = \\ln y\\,\\mathbf i - (x+y)\\,\\mathbf j + z\\,\\mathbf k$\n3. $\\mathbf C(t) = \\sin{t}\\,\\mathbf i + (2-t^3)\\,\\mathbf j$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of $\\mathbb R^2$. A vector field on $\\mathbb R^2$ is a function $\\mathbf F$ that maps every point $(x,y)$ in $S$ to a vector $\\mathbf F(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R^2$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $\\mathbf A(x,y)$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2$. It maps every point in the set $S$, given by\n$S = \\big\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2 \\: \\mid \\: x \\geq 0\\big\\}$\nto a vector in $\\mathbb R^2$.\n- The function $\\mathbf B(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2$. It is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$.\n- The function $\\mathbf C(t)$ is *not* a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2$. It assigns points in $\\mathbb{R}$ (not $\\mathbb{R^2}$) to vectors in $\\mathbb{R^2}$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171824 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "67b7ea90-38e0-54b5-b76e-816beb0d48f9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain of a Vector Field"}] :question/id #uuid "193765c8-ea2c-5d90-b902-09d6d44a3a67" :question/math-academy-id "e-9466" :question/problem "Find the domain of the vector field $\\mathbf F$ on $\\mathbb R^2$, given by\n\n$\\mathbf F(x,y) =\\dfrac{1}{x^2+y^2}\\, \\mathbf{i} + \\sqrt{1+x-y} \\, \\mathbf{j}\\,$." :question/worked-solution "The function $\\mathbf F$ can be expressed in terms of its components $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y) = P(x,y)\\,\\mathbf i + Q(x,y)\\,\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y) =\\dfrac{1}{x^2+y^2}, \\qquad Q(x,y) = \\sqrt{1+x-y}\n$$\n\nWe first find the domain of each component function. Then, we compute their intersection to find the domain of $\\mathbf F$:\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n$x^2 + y^2 = 0 \\quad\\Longrightarrow \\quad x=y = 0$\nTherefore, the domain of $P$ is given by\n$D_1 = \\big\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\: \\mid \\: (x,y)\\neq (0,0)\\big\\}$.\nThe region $D_1$ is the entire plane $\\mathbb R^2$ with the origin removed, as shown below.![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3343/Source/Images/2d11f66345e024eed7bf280882ed66a0.png)\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined when the expression under the radical is positive or zero. To find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n$1+x - y \\geq 0 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad y\\leq 1 + x$\nTherefore, the domain of $Q$ is given by\n$D_2 = \\big\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\: \\mid \\: y \\leq 1 + x\\big\\}$.\n\nThe region $D_2$, shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3343/Source/Images/4792228f471aa7edfa5498ff57e5d63b.png)\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $\\mathbf F$ is given by\n\n$$\nD = D_1\\cap D_2 = \\big\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\: \\mid \\: (x,y)\\neq 0,\\, y \\leq 1 + x\\big\\}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3343/Source/Images/fd17dd94e4234f271b92358cae0a9b6d.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171825 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb34fdb0-198e-507d-a944-9be782b4cfeb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Fields on Three-Dimensions"}] :question/id #uuid "61c3506a-5e4e-557f-bb8c-077170e93204" :question/math-academy-id "e-9468" :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on $\\mathbb R^3$?\n\n1. $\\mathbf R(x,y,z) = 2\\,\\mathbf i - x\\,\\mathbf j + y\\,\\mathbf k$\n2. $\\mathbf S(x,y) = 2x\\,\\mathbf i - y^3\\,\\mathbf j$\n3. $\\mathbf T(x,y,z) = \\dfrac{x}{z}\\,\\mathbf i - \\dfrac{y}{z}\\,\\mathbf j + \\dfrac{z}{2}\\,\\mathbf k$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of $\\mathbb R^3$. A vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$ is a function $\\mathbf F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)\\in\\mathbb R^3$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $\\mathbf R(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$. It assigns every point $(x,y,z) \\in \\mathbb{R^3}$ to a vector in $\\mathbb{R^3}$.\n- The function $\\mathbf S(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$. It assigns points in $\\mathbb R^2$ (not $\\mathbb R^3$) to vectors in $\\mathbb R^2$ (not $\\mathbb R^3$).\n- The function $\\mathbf T(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$. It assigns every point in the set $S$, given by\n$S = \\big\\{(x,y,z)\\in\\mathbb R^3\\: \\mid \\: z \\neq 0\\big\\}$\nto a vector in $\\mathbb R^3$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172210 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "019e5a17-1aab-565b-8881-447c86a9a7ec" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Fields on Two-Dimensions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8aafae1c-0179-537d-bb93-ac3e28cd5106" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168464} {:answer/id #uuid "c181a59f-0de2-5022-a0a7-6b53acc98b9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168465} {:answer/id #uuid "d1cceee4-bbd6-5945-b02f-a2d18db0f803" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168466} {:answer/id #uuid "d423743f-39ec-5068-bcb1-ce3429eb332f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168467} {:answer/id #uuid "e2809bc9-2114-59ca-8d8f-a14c63b49225" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168468}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d423743f-39ec-5068-bcb1-ce3429eb332f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168467} :answer-field/id #uuid "ecf52b9b-b711-5cf2-880f-ec86893ec28a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168463}] :question/id #uuid "b2d1a20d-340e-5980-ad18-92b97e0270ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-122465" :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on $R^{2}$?\n\n1. $\\mathbf{G} ( x , y ) = {x}^{2} \\, \\mathbf{i} - {y}^{2} \\, \\mathbf{j}$\n2. $\\mathbf{H} ( x , y ) = \\ln   y \\, \\mathbf{i} - {e}^{x y} \\, \\mathbf{j}$\n3. $\\mathbf{K} ( x , y , z ) = x \\, \\mathbf{i} - y \\, \\mathbf{j} + z \\, \\mathbf{k}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172211 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb34fdb0-198e-507d-a944-9be782b4cfeb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Fields on Three-Dimensions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce32078b-519c-54b1-b8ce-727c3b937980" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168470} {:answer/id #uuid "070f08fc-4f6d-577b-a74c-d4e38513ffce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168471} {:answer/id #uuid "0a497576-22df-5a0d-b830-1f7d8688d19f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168472} {:answer/id #uuid "0d248294-2217-5669-a332-d4678fda8e9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168473} {:answer/id #uuid "1c7e461d-72ef-5430-a20c-68c1551911b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168474}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1c7e461d-72ef-5430-a20c-68c1551911b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168474} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc46bc05-4327-54c6-96b9-644fb88d57ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168469}] :question/id #uuid "060c20fc-1491-5624-9b50-89ab0dfe4d22" :question/math-academy-id "q-122467" :question/problem "Which of the following are vector fields on $R^{3}$?\n\n1. $\\mathbf{G} ( t ) = {e}^{t} \\, \\mathbf{i} - t \\, \\mathbf{j} + \\sin   t \\, \\mathbf{k}$\n2. $\\mathbf{H} ( x , y ) = {x}^{2} \\, \\mathbf{i} - {y}^{2} \\, \\mathbf{j}$\n3. $\\mathbf{K} ( x , y , z ) = \\cot   x \\, \\mathbf{i} - y \\, \\mathbf{j} + z \\, \\mathbf{k}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172213 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "67b7ea90-38e0-54b5-b76e-816beb0d48f9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain of a Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c3f51021-bc37-512e-9007-b21321c31ac0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3343/Source/Images/q-122518-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168482} {:answer/id #uuid "d06346a3-0f5d-5c1a-b0fb-ffcbff145ac3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3343/Source/Images/q-122518-a-3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168483} {:answer/id #uuid "078b30e7-95e6-50ae-b28e-39e6d1dbe152" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3343/Source/Images/q-122518-a-5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168484} {:answer/id #uuid "221365d6-2e30-5cb0-9160-554e42fe2c13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3343/Source/Images/q-122518-a-4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168485} {:answer/id #uuid "3a7dc72